# This file is a signpost, not a template.
#
# The environment template lives at `apps/web/.env.example`, and that is the
# only one. Copy it to `apps/web/.env.local`:
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env.local
#
# Why not here: Next.js loads `.env.local` from the application directory, not
# from the workspace root. A value written into a root `.env.local` in this
# monorepo is read by nothing, and there is no error to tell you so.
#
# This file used to be a full second template, and it was the more dangerous
# of the two, because it was both unread by Next.js and the more out of date.
# It declared these, none of which any `process.env` reference in the codebase
# reads: AUTH_DATABASE_URL, GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET, four
# APPLE_* keys, X_CLIENT_ID, X_CLIENT_SECRET, three CLOUDINARY_* keys,
# GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_KEY, NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_BROWSER_KEY,
# TRAVELGATE_API_KEY, TERMII_API_KEY, SENTRY_DSN, NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN,
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYSTACK_PUBLIC_KEY, and a PAYSTACK_WEBHOOK_SECRET that Paystack
# does not issue at all. Setting any of them configures nothing, silently.
#
# Two templates cannot both be right, and an operator reading the wrong one
# configures a capability that does not exist. So there is now one.
# `docs/ENVIRONMENT.md` says what each absence costs.
#
# The social sign-in secrets in particular are not environment variables of
# this application at all: they are pasted into the Supabase dashboard, under
# Authentication > Providers. This app only reads NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDERS,
# which decides which buttons to draw.
